## 1. Product Overview

HealthyLife AI is an AI-powered health and nutrition platform that helps users monitor their daily nutrition, understand their eating habits, and move toward a healthier lifestyle through personalized recommendations. Users record meals, get their daily nutritional needs calculated automatically, track health progress (including body weight) over time, and receive AI-generated insights based on their personal health profile and dietary behavior.

HealthyLife AI provides wellness and nutrition guidance only. **It does not provide medical diagnosis or replace professional healthcare advice.**

## 2. Problem Statement

Maintaining a healthy lifestyle is increasingly difficult due to unhealthy eating habits, low nutritional awareness, and a lack of personalized guidance. Many existing nutrition apps let users log meals or count calories, but most stop at data recording rather than turning that data into meaningful, personalized insight that actually changes behavior.

## 3. Goals & Objectives

**Main objective:** design, develop, and deploy an AI-powered health and nutrition platform within six Agile sprints that lets users monitor nutritional habits, calculate daily nutritional requirements, track health progress, and receive personalized AI-generated recommendations — through responsive web and mobile applications supporting Arabic and English.

**Specific objectives (SMART, by sprint):**

| # | Objective | Target |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Secure user authentication (register, log in) | End of Sprint 1 |
| 2 | Personal & health profile creation with full validation | End of Sprint 2 |
| 3 | Accurate daily calorie & macronutrient calculation from the health profile | During Sprint 2 |
| 4 | Record, edit, search, and manage daily meals with nutritional calculation | End of Sprint 3 |
| 5 | Dashboards and interactive charts for nutrition/health progress | End of Sprint 4 |
| 6 | Personalized AI-powered health recommendations from meal history + profile | During Sprint 5 |
| 7 | Seamless Arabic/English switching across web and mobile | Before project completion |
| 8 | Responsive web + mobile apps integrated with a centralized backend | End of project |
| 9 | Administrator dashboard for users, food categories, food database | Before final deployment |
| 10 | Scalable, maintainable, fully tested & documented architecture | End of Sprint 6 |

## 4. Success Metrics

Pulled from the SRS's non-functional targets (see §8 for the full list) and the User Story catalog's Definition of Done — these are the metrics a `/speckit.analyze` pass or a UAT plan should check against:

- ≥ 90% of test users complete their primary task without assistance; a new user completes registration + health profile in ≤ 5 minutes (NFR-03).
- ≤ 3 second response time under up to 100 concurrent users; primary screens load in ≤ 5 seconds (NFR-01).
- ≤ 1% failure rate on critical operations (NFR-04).
- ≥ 99% availability during normal service hours (NFR-05).
- ≥ 80% unit test coverage on critical business components (NFR-07).
- AI recommendation and nutrition-calculation requests complete in ≤ 5 seconds under normal load.

