## 14. Definition of Ready / Definition of Done

**Definition of Ready** (before a story enters a sprint):
- The related FR is approved and unambiguous.
- The story follows "As a / I want / so that" and fits within one sprint.
- Dependencies on other stories are identified and either done or explicitly accepted as parallel work.
- Acceptance criteria are written and peer-reviewed.
- Any required UI/UX reference (mockup/wireframe) is available.
- The team has agreed on an estimate.

**Definition of Done** (before a story is considered complete, regardless of individual AC pass status):
- Code is merged to main and passes CI.
- All acceptance criteria pass manual or automated testing.
- Unit tests exist for new logic and meet the coverage target (NFR-07, ≥80% on critical components).
- The feature works correctly in both Arabic and English where UI is involved (FR-09).
- The feature works correctly on both web and mobile clients where applicable (FR-10) — currently web-only until the mobile app exists (§7.1).
- No known critical/high-severity defects remain open against the story.
- Relevant documentation (API reference, in-app help text) is updated.
- The Product Owner/Supervisor has reviewed and accepted the demonstrated feature.

## 15. Assumptions, Risks & Open Questions

### Assumptions
- Users have basic smartphone/web literacy and a reasonably stable internet connection.
- The food database is seeded with representative Arabic + international foods before Sprint 3 begins.
- The AI recommendation service (internal model or external API) is available and licensed before Sprint 5 begins.
- Team availability holds across all six sprints as planned.

### Constraints
- Six-sprint internship timeline caps scope to an MVP.
- Small student development team, limited weekly hours.
- AI recommendations rely on predefined health/nutrition data, not medical diagnosis or licensed clinical advice.
- UAT uses a limited number of test users — may not represent all real-world usage.

### Open Questions (resolve with `/speckit.clarify` before planning the affected epic)
1. **Weight-log routes** — is `POST /weight-logs` (create) and `GET /weight-logs` (list) the correct contract, or does the backend use different paths/verbs? (§7.2) — *blocks finalizing Epic 2's spec as "done," not just "implemented as a best guess."*
2. **Daily nutrition calculation endpoint** — will the backend expose `GET/POST /nutrition/daily-needs` (or similar), and if so, what formula/rounding rules should the frontend match exactly? (§7.2) — *blocks Epic 2 US-03 from being fully backend-verified.*
3. **Password reset (FR-01d)** — not yet scoped for the frontend or confirmed in the backend's API surface. Needs its own mini-spec before it can be scheduled.
4. **Mobile app stack** — SRS assumed Flutter; nothing has been started. Confirm before Sprint 3/4 planning whether Flutter is still the intended stack, given the web pivot from React to Next.js.
5. **AI service selection** — which external AI API (or internal model) will power US-08a/US-08b, and what are its cost/rate-limit/availability characteristics? Needed before Sprint 5 planning.
6. **Admin role model** — how is the `admin` role represented in the Laravel backend (a field on `User`, a separate table, Laravel policies/gates)? Needed before Epic 6 planning.

## 16. Document Provenance & Reconciliation Notes

This PRD consolidates three source documents plus the current codebase:

- **SRS — Software Requirements Specification, Chapters 1–2 (Revised).** Source for §3 (objectives), §6 (scope), §8 (NFRs), §5 (stakeholders), and the FR numbering used throughout. Its technology chapter (§1.8–§1.9) is **explicitly superseded** by §7.1 of this PRD.
- **Milestones (Aligned, v1.2).** Source for the sprint/milestone structure (§10), and for the Sprint 1–2 story split used in §11.1–§11.2 (`US-01a`/`US-01b` instead of a single combined `US-01`), since that split matches what was actually built.
- **User Stories & Use Case Scenarios (Aligned, v2.3).** Source for the detailed Use Case flows (main/alternative/exception), the NFR-driven cross-cutting stories (§12, US-13–US-20), the admin/testing/deployment stories (US-11, US-21, US-22), and the Traceability Matrix skeleton (§13).

**Known discrepancy between sources:** the Milestones document (v1.2) states it is cross-checked against User Stories v2.3, but the two documents assign different story-point totals to Sprint 1/2 (Milestones: `US-01a`(5) + `US-01b`(5) = 10 pts for Sprint 1, `US-02`(5)+`US-03`(3)+`US-12`(3) = 11 pts for Sprint 2; User Stories catalog: single `US-01`(8) covering register/login/logout/reset, `US-02`(5)+`US-03`(5)+`US-12`(3) = 13 pts for Sprint 2). This PRD resolves the discrepancy by **using the Milestones document's story split** (since it matches the shipped implementation) while **using the User Story catalog's richer acceptance-criteria and use-case detail** to fill in each story. If this matters for velocity tracking, treat the User Story catalog's `US-01` as the parent/epic-level record and `US-01a`/`US-01b` as its two implementation-level sub-stories.

